riscos-mesa: NEON in the GLSL/ARB batch interpreter (TEST BUILD, not
in the release build list yet).

While every lane of a batch is active and the batch is a multiple of four
fragments, runs of plain instructions (no relative addressing, negation
or saturation) with ABS ADD CMP DP2 DP3 DP4 LRP MAD MAX MIN MOV MUL SGE
SLT SUB, whose destination is a temporary or output and not one of its
sources, are run four lanes at a time with NEON (ro_neon_run): the same
IEEE operations in the same order as the C.

NEON flushes denormals to zero and gives the default NaN, where VFP (as
UnixLib sets it up) keeps denormals and passes NaN operands through. So
each group checks its operands for 0 < |x| < 2^-30 (with operands of 0
or at least 2^-30 no product or sum of products here can be a denormal)
and its arithmetic results for NaN; if any lane hits one, the C code runs
the instruction again for every lane. Output is bit-identical to the C
code (checked under qemu-arm: the fuzz shader sets, edge cases with
denormals and NaNs, glbench's scenes).

Used only when VFPSupport_Features reports Advanced SIMD (MVFR1 SIMD
load/store, integer and single precision); the system variable
MESA_NO_NEON turns it off. GCC compiles only ro_neon_run with NEON
(#pragma GCC target), so the rest still runs on VFPv3-only machines.

